--TEST--
`instanceof` over a subject whose declared type can hold no object is `E0497` where it is written — a scalar, an `array<T>`, an enum and a union naming no class — ADR 0007 § 7 row 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
enum Mode: int {
    Read = 1,
    Write = 2,
}

final class Box {
    public int $n = 1;
}

// ADR 0007 § 7 row 14: a declared scalar already answered, so the test is dead
// code that reads as a live question. PHP has no declaration to read and
// answers `false`; refusing where it is written is the same move `rule:expressions/one-equality-operator`
// makes for two statically disjoint types under `==`.
int $n = 1;
bool $a = $n instanceof Box;
string $s = "Box";
bool $b = $s instanceof Box;
// An `array<T>` is a value, never an object — `rule:iteration/two-interfaces` gave iteration its own
// interfaces rather than making one.
array<string> $rows = ["a"];
bool $c = $rows instanceof Box;
// An enum case is a named integer (`rule:enums/closed-integer-type`), on the subject side as well as
// on the class side.
Mode $mode = Mode::Read;
bool $d = $mode instanceof Box;
// A union naming no class at all is refused for the same reason each of its
// members would be — while `?Box` is a union that *does* name one, and keeps
// its run-time test.
int|string $u = 5;
bool $e = $u instanceof Box;
echo "unreached\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0497]: `int` can never be an object, so `instanceof` cannot ask
   --> %s:16:11
   |
16 | bool $a = $n instanceof Box;
   |           ^^ this value's type already answers
   = help: declare the subject `mixed`, `object`, or the base class you expect — a declared scalar, `array<T>` or enum is not a class and never becomes one (ADR 0007 § 7 row 14)

